WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.618
A bowl. A ball.

00:00:02.697 --> 00:00:06.392
Watch it step down on my count.
One. Two, three.

00:00:06.551 --> 00:00:11.210
I wrote the words and drew the pictures,
and each picture waits for its word.

00:00:12.214 --> 00:00:16.489
You just watched DeckTalk.
You write a script and simple slides.

00:00:16.881 --> 00:00:21.156
It reads your script aloud, and you
pick the word each picture waits for.

00:00:21.525 --> 00:00:26.393
Change the script, and the video follows.
Here is a lesson I made with it.

00:00:27.234 --> 00:00:31.927
How does AI learn?
From examples, not rules people write.

00:00:32.099 --> 00:00:34.899
Learning from examples
is called machine learning.

00:00:35.198 --> 00:00:37.813
An AI model is like a box of knobs.

00:00:37.892 --> 00:00:42.643
It guesses, and a meter shows its error,
how wrong the guess was.

00:00:43.117 --> 00:00:48.297
Learning turns the knobs until the meter
drops, example after example.

00:00:49.142 --> 00:00:51.374
But which way should each knob turn?

00:00:51.894 --> 00:00:54.634
In a big model,
testing knobs one at a time

00:00:54.634 --> 00:00:57.388
would take billions
of tries for every step.

00:00:57.815 --> 00:01:02.311
Instead, one round of math gives
a direction for every knob at once.

00:01:03.272 --> 00:01:06.781
Picture two knobs.
The floor is every way to set them.

00:01:07.196 --> 00:01:11.622
The height is the error.
For this lesson it is a bowl.

00:01:13.512 --> 00:01:16.660
The ball is where the knobs
are set right now.

00:01:17.122 --> 00:01:21.270
It can't see the whole bowl,
only the slope where it stands.

00:01:21.708 --> 00:01:27.899
So it steps downhill, again and again, and
the steps shrink as the ground flattens.

00:01:28.198 --> 00:01:33.135
That is gradient descent.
It's what the ball did on my count.

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:37.605
One setting makes every
step longer or shorter.

00:01:37.985 --> 00:01:43.317
Too long, and it bounces past the bottom.
Too short, and it is very slow.

00:01:44.127 --> 00:01:47.067
Zoom out, and the ground
can have many valleys.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.945
The ball finds a low one,
not always the lowest.

00:01:52.951 --> 00:01:56.216
Today's AI is trained
with a version of this.

00:01:56.538 --> 00:02:00.036
Inside, the knobs are grids of numbers,
called tensors.

00:02:01.078 --> 00:02:06.735
Chips like GPUs and TPUs multiply grids,
row times column, all at once.

00:02:07.312 --> 00:02:08.822
For a big model, the chips do

00:02:08.822 --> 00:02:12.598
billions of billions of small
calculations for every step.

00:02:13.164 --> 00:02:15.974
The large language models
behind AI chat apps

00:02:15.974 --> 00:02:18.704
split the work across thousands of chips.

00:02:19.457 --> 00:02:20.621
That's the lesson.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:25.381
Now I'll change one sentence, the kind
of edit that used to mean recording again.

00:02:25.784 --> 00:02:28.678
Say a viewer asks why downhill is good.

00:02:28.942 --> 00:02:32.335
So I add four words,
and make the video again.

00:02:32.681 --> 00:02:35.668
Nothing re-recorded or re-edited by hand.

00:02:36.187 --> 00:02:39.081
The height is the error,
so lower is better.

00:02:39.427 --> 00:02:42.366
Every later picture
moved to stay on its word.

00:02:43.144 --> 00:02:45.654
Update your video the way
you update a doc.

00:02:46.151 --> 00:02:50.008
DeckTalk. Open source,
narrated presentations, cut to the word.

00:02:50.168 --> 00:02:52.493
Make your own at decktalk dot app.
